/* The handoff's stat cell, quoted from the region detail card's three-cell
 * row - "flex: 1; background: #f8f6fc; border-radius: 12px; padding: 9px
 * 10px", with a 9px uppercase micro label over a 12px bold value.
 *
 * WHAT IS TOKENISED AND WHAT IS QUOTED, on ScreenScaffold.uss's convention:
 * the token layer holds the SCALE, and one-off component geometry taken from
 * the handoff is written literally next to the sentence it came from.
 *   - `#f8f6fc` IS --surface-sunk exactly, so it is the token.
 *   - the handoff's per-screen radii range over the 12-16px band it declares
 *     for rows; --radius-row (14px) is the token layer's single answer for
 *     that band, so this is the scale rather than one-off geometry.
 *   - 9px and 10px padding are not on the spacing scale (8 and 12 sit either
 *     side) and 3px is the gap under the label. Rounding them to steps the
 *     handoff did not ask for would change the cell's proportion for the
 *     sake of tidiness, so they are literal, as the scaffold's own 14/10/34
 *     are.
 *
 * THE VALUE'S SIZE IS NOT SET HERE, AND THAT IS A DECISION. `t-num` in
 * Theme.uss sets --text-secondary (11px); the handoff's inline style for
 * this cell says 12px. Overriding it from this file would put the 11px
 * numeral floor - one of bible 10.6's two hard requirements - at the mercy
 * of which single-class rule the cascade happened to apply last, for one
 * pixel. The floor wins; the 1px is recorded here rather than argued with
 * silently.
 */
.stat-cell {
    /* The handoff's `flex: 1`. flex-basis 0 rather than the default auto, so
     * three cells come out equal width instead of sized by their content. */
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding-top: 9px;
    padding-bottom: 9px;
    padding-left: 10px;
    padding-right: 10px;
    /* HALF THE HANDOFF'S 8px GAP ON EACH SIDE, because a cell cannot own a
     * gap any other way. USS has no `gap` and no `:last-child`, so a single
     * right margin would leak past the last cell in the row - and styling
     * the ROW from this file is not available either: see the note at the
     * foot of this sheet. The cost is 4px of dead space at each end of a
     * row; a caller that wants it flush cancels it with a -4px margin on the
     * container, which is one line at the call site rather than a rule that
     * looks like it works and does not. */
    margin-left: var(--space-1);
    margin-right: var(--space-1);
}

/* HEIGHT ON THE BOX, NEVER ON THE LABEL'S OWN MEASUREMENT - Phase 9 Task
 * 16b's rule, and this cell is the FIFTH instance of the defect it was written
 * for. `.chip` was the first, the splice chamber's `PARENT A` eyebrow the
 * second, and `.deploy-view__pill-label`/`-value` the third and fourth.
 *
 * MEASURED, NOT REASONED. On the first Task 18 capture of
 * `WaveDefeatView.png` at the 430 frame, a cell's white surface ran
 * y=171..248 - 78px - against the handoff's 50 (`Wave Defeat.dc.html:44-46`:
 * `padding: 9px 11px`, a 9px `.lbl` and a 19px value at `line-height: 1.05`).
 * 78 is 9 + 9 of padding, 3 of `__value-line`'s own margin, and FIFTY-SEVEN
 * for two Labels that measure 28.6 between them: exactly 16b's shape, a Label
 * that is a CHILD of a column rather than the box itself, measuring 28.6
 * where its own line box is 14.
 *
 * *** "TWO LINE BOXES" WAS THE WRONG MODEL, AND TASK 20 MEASURED IT. ***
 * `LabelBoxProbe` (implementation/scripts/probe-label-box.sh) reports, on a
 * real panel: a bare Label at 10px carries resolvedStyle padding 4/4 and
 * margin 4/2 FROM UNITY'S OWN DEFAULT RUNTIME THEME, around a 13.6 -> 14
 * line box. Its border box is 4 + 14 + 4 = 22; its MARGIN box is
 * 4 + 22 + 2 = 28. So 28 is not two line boxes - it is 14px of fixed theme
 * chrome around ONE. The two readings agree to within 0.8px (2 x 13.6 =
 * 27.2), which is exactly why the wrong model survived six sightings.
 *
 * THAT IS ALSO WHY THE OVERHEAD IS NOT LINEAR: the chrome is a CONSTANT
 * 14px, independent of font-size, so it cannot scale with the line box. The
 * parent is irrelevant - the probe measured the identical Label at 22px in a
 * row parent, a column parent, stretched, unstretched, and with
 * `white-space: nowrap`. Zeroing the Label's own padding and margin yields
 * 14, the bare line box; that turns `TraitPip` from 34px into 20px, which is
 * `TraitChip`'s height exactly.
 *
 * 14 AND 15 ARE THE AUTHORED LINE BOXES, ROUNDED UP. Nunito-Bold SDF is
 * m_LineHeight 122.76 on m_PointSize 90 = 1.36em (`Nunito-Bold SDF
 * .asset:149,152`, measured in Theme.uss's chip block): --text-micro (10px) x
 * 1.36 = 13.6 -> 14, and `t-num`'s --text-secondary (11px) x 1.36 = 15.0.
 * Up rather than down, because a height UNDER the line box clips descenders.
 * 9 + 14 + 3 + 15 + 9 = 50, which is the handoff's number for this cell.
 *
 * `-unity-text-align: middle-left` IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE RULE. A height on
 * its own pins the box and leaves the glyphs hanging from its top edge.
 *
 * THE VALUE'S HEIGHT IS ON THE ROW AS WELL AS ON THE LABEL, and the row is the
 * one that moved the number. Measured across three captures of the same
 * fixture: 78 with neither, 62 with the Label's height alone, 56 with both.
 * `__value-line` is the BOX the value Label is a child of, which is exactly
 * what 16b's rule names; the Label's own height stays because the row holds a
 * trend arrow too and a bare row height would let a taller Label push out of
 * it.
 *
 * AND 56 IS NOT 50. The residual is 6px per cell and it is the SAME 6px Task
 * 17 measured and could not account for either ("the last 6px to 51 came from
 * pinning the surface, and no declaration in the sheet accounts for it" -
 * `DeployView.uss`'s pill note, report §6.6). Probed here rather than left as
 * a shrug: with both heights set to 0 the cell measures 13px where 9 + 3 + 9
 * of padding and margin predict 21, so the overhead is NOT a constant added
 * to each box and the relationship is not linear. The block above has the
 * mechanism; this cell's own 13-vs-21 figure was NOT re-measured by Task 20's
 * probe and is left as the capture recorded it.
 *
 * IT REACHES FOUR SCREENS AND THAT IS THE POINT. `RegionView`, `DeployView`,
 * `PostWaveView` and `WaveDefeatView` all draw this cell, and all four were
 * 28px over per cell. Fixing it on one screen's sheet would have left the
 * other three wrong and the component still carrying the defect - which is
 * `--chip-height`'s own argument, one component over.
 *
 * NO FONT-SIZE HERE, WHICH IS UNCHANGED. `t-micro` still carries all four of
 * the handoff's values for the label - 10px, --mute, bold, the uppercase
 * tracking - and the value's size and tabular face still come from `t-num`.
 * This rule sets a box, not type.
 *
 * The value's own rule is layout plus the handoff's ink; its size and its
 * tabular face come from `t-num`, per the note at the head of this file. */
/* THE VALUE IS A ROW NOW, NOT A LABEL, so a trend arrow can sit beside the
 * number instead of under it - the handoff's `B+ ▲` and `C ▼`. The 3px that
 * used to be the value's own top margin moves here, because the row is what
 * follows the label.
 *
 * `align-items: center` rather than `baseline`: the arrow is a background
 * image with no baseline of its own, and a flex item without one is aligned
 * by its bottom edge, which drops a triangle below the digits it belongs to. */
.stat-cell__value-line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-top: 3px;
    height: 15px;
}

.stat-cell__label {
    height: 14px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.stat-cell__value {
    color: var(--ink-primary);
    height: 15px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* TWO CLASSES, to beat icons.uss's 19px without depending on which sheet the
 * panel applies last - the rule every override of a Theme/icons class in
 * this phase follows. 11px is the handoff's own 10px arrow rounded to the
 * nearest whole pixel a 57px raster halves cleanly into, and it sits beside
 * an 11px value.
 *
 * NO TINT HERE. `icon--trend-up` and `icon--trend-down` carry their own
 * green and coral in icons.uss, so a cell that forgot one would get
 * `.icon`'s --mute-soft and read as a disabled arrow rather than as no
 * arrow. Setting a colour here as well would mean two files deciding it. */
.stat-cell .stat-cell__trend {
    width: 11px;
    height: 11px;
    margin-left: var(--space-1);
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* THERE IS NO `.stat-cell-row` RULE IN THIS FILE, AND THERE CANNOT BE ONE.
 *
 * The first version of this component had one - `flex-direction: row` plus
 * a -4px margin on each side to cancel the cells' own - and it compiled, it
 * passed every gate, and it styled nothing at all. A stylesheet attached
 * through `<ui:Style>` in a component's UXML lands on the element the tree
 * is cloned into and applies to THAT ELEMENT AND ITS DESCENDANTS. The
 * container holding a row of cells is the cells' PARENT, so it is outside
 * every sheet the cells carry, and the rule sat inert with nothing to say
 * so. The Components capture is what caught it: the three cells rendered
 * stacked and full width, at zero height, with their values spilling out
 * of the card below them - a flex-basis of 0 in a container that was still
 * a column because the rule that would have made it a row never arrived.
 *
 * WORTH KNOWING BEFORE TASKS 9-12 WRITE ANOTHER ONE. A component's own
 * stylesheet can describe the component and everything inside it, and can
 * describe NOTHING about where it is placed. Layout that belongs to the
 * container - a row of cells, a grid of cards, a gap between siblings - is
 * the container's to own, in the stylesheet of whatever owns the container.
 */
